  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 200 PM CDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nMid and upper level high pressure centered over the southeastern\nU.S. will continue to be the main influence on our weather across\nsouth central Texas through the end of the week and into early\nnext week. A surge of low-level moisture on Thursday into Friday\nalong with daytime heating should aid in the development of\nisolated to scattered convection for areas generally along and\neast of the I-35 corridor. The NBM pops look a bit high, but did\nopt to keep scattered rain chances (30-50%) over the coastal\nplains with rain chances closer to 20% farther west into the I-35\ncorridor. \n\nMoisture levels decrease rather quickly for the upcoming weekend\nas the high pressure over the southeastern U.S. builds into the\nsouthern U.S. plains. The latest NBM shows some rain chances\nreturning to a good portion of the region early next week as high\npressure begins to weaken. Given the current suite of operational\nmodels, we are a bit skeptical of this. However, if a weak frontal\nboundary can manage to slide southward early next week, our rain\nchances would improve. Temperatures continue to remain above\nnormal through the period.",
